\paragraph{Why test-time scaling fails for NER.}
For discrete-answer reasoning, sample diversity reflects diverse reasoning paths; for NER, output convergence (degeneracy $12$--$68$\%) eliminates candidate diversity while signal alignment collapse ($\alpha{\to}0$) prevents ranking---a \textit{capability-diversity tension} observed across model families (5.5--74\%; Table~\ref{tab:frontier_sc_cross}) and scales (4B--72B; \S\ref{sec:scaling}).
Free-form decoding confirms this is model-intrinsic rather than decoder-induced (within-instance LP std ratio $0.97{\times}$; \S\ref{sec:selection_gap}).

\paragraph{Practical implications.}
The framework provides a pre-deployment diagnostic: measure degeneracy and LP-F1 alignment with $N{=}8$ samples before investing in $N{\times}$ inference; when degeneracy exceeds $\theta_{\text{crit}}{=}0.70$, aggregation is unlikely to help.
Thresholds are empirically stable across our NER datasets but may require task-specific calibration; generalization beyond 7--14B fine-tuned NER requires dedicated investigation.

\paragraph{Conclusion.}
We characterize when test-time scaling fails for NER, with exploratory RE and SQL extensions.
The diagnostic framework---localizing failure to output convergence and signal alignment collapse---is our primary contribution; whether the capability-diversity tension extends beyond NER remains open.

